Numerical Methods I — Florian Schaefer. Source: Lecture 03.pdf, pp. 1–8.3.1 RecapThe preceding lecture discussed array storage, the distinction between arithmetic work and memory-access cost, cache, loop ordering, and tiling. It then introduced triangular substitution and Gaussian elimination for linear systems.The question now is how to organize elimination into a factorization that can be stored and used efficiently.3.2 Elementary Gaussian EliminationAt step k, let A denote the current matrix after the first k-1 elimination steps. Its first k-1 columns are already zero below their diagonals. Assume A_{kk}
e0.The elementary lower-triangular matrix L_k has ones on the diagonal and (L_k)_{ik}=- rac{A_{ik}}{A_{kk}},\qquad i=k+1,\ldots,m, with all other off-diagonal entries zero. Multiplication by L_k performs  egin{aligned} (L_kA)_{ik} &=A_{ik}- rac{A_{ik}}{A_{kk}}A_{kk}=0, &&i>k,\ (L_kA)_{ij} &=A_{ij}- rac{A_{ik}}{A_{kk}}A_{kj}, &&i>k,\ j>k. \end{aligned} This is Theorem 1 from the preceding lecture: each step creates zeros below one more diagonal entry and updates the trailing block.3.3 LU FactorizationIf all the required elimination steps are defined, then U=L_m\cdots L_1A is upper triangular. As in the source, the product includes a final, trivial factor L_m=I. The corresponding transformed system isLecture 03, p. 3 repeats the transformed-system line from Lecture 02 without its vector x. The missing vector is restored here. \underbrace{L_m\cdots L_1A}_{U}x=L_m\cdots L_1b. Define L=(L_m\cdots L_1)^{-1} =L_1^{-1}\cdots L_m^{-1}. Then  oxed{A=LU.} To make this useful, we need to show that L is lower triangular and understand its entries.3.4 Algebra of Lower-Triangular MatricesA matrix L\in\mathbb{K}^{m	imes m} is lower triangular when L_{ij}=0 for i<j.[!theorem] Theorem 2 — Closure properties Lower-triangular matrices are closed under addition, scalar multiplication, and matrix multiplication. The inverse of a nonsingular lower-triangular matrix is also lower triangular.Addition and scalar multiplication preserve the zeros above the diagonal immediately.For multiplication, let L and \widetilde{L} be lower triangular. Then (L\widetilde{L})_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^m L_{ik}\widetilde{L}_{kj}. A nonzero summand requires both L_{ik}
e0\Longrightarrow i\ge k, \qquad \widetilde{L}_{kj}
e0\Longrightarrow k\ge j. Consequently, L_{ik}\widetilde{L}_{kj}
e0\Longrightarrow i\ge k\ge j. If i<j, no such k exists, so (L\widetilde{L})_{ij}=0. Hence the product is lower triangular.The inversion property is completed after analyzing elementary lower-triangular matrices.3.5 Elementary Lower-Triangular MatricesWrite the elimination multipliers as \ell_{ik} and define \ell_k=  egin{pmatrix} 0\ \vdots\ 0\ \ell_{k+1,k}\ \vdots\ \ell_{mk} \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \underbrace{(\ell_k)_1=\cdots=(\ell_k)_k}_{	ext{first }k	ext{ entries}}=0. Let e_k be the k-th coordinate vector. The star denotes conjugate transpose, so e_k^*\ell_k selects the k-th entry of \ell_k. Then L_k=I-\ell_ke_k^*, \qquad e_k^*\ell_k=0. [!theorem] Theorem 3 — Inverses and products of elementary factors For L_k=I-\ell_ke_k^* with (\ell_k)_i=0 for i\le k, L_k^{-1}=I+\ell_ke_k^*. Moreover, in the indicated order, L_1^{-1}\cdots L_m^{-1} =I+\sum_{k=1}^m\ell_ke_k^* = egin{pmatrix} 1&0&\cdots&0\ \ell_{21}&1&\ddots&\vdots\ \vdots&\ddots&\ddots&0\ \ell_{m1}&\cdots&\ell_{m,m-1}&1 \end{pmatrix}. 3.5.1 Proof of the Inverse FormulaExpand the product:  egin{aligned} (I+\ell_ke_k^*)(I-\ell_ke_k^*) &=I+\cancel{\ell_ke_k^*}-\cancel{\ell_ke_k^*} -\ell_k\underbrace{(e_k^*\ell_k)}_{0}e_k^*\ &=I. \end{aligned} Thus changing the signs of the multipliers gives the inverse of an elementary elimination matrix.3.5.2 Proof of the Product FormulaThe lecture proves the result entrywise. Consider  egin{aligned} (L_1^{-1}\cdots L_m^{-1})_{ij} &=e_i^*(I+\ell_1e_1^*)\cdots(I+\ell_me_m^*)e_j\ &=e_i^*(I+\ell_1e_1^*)\cdots (I+\ell_{j-1}e_{j-1}^*)(e_j+\ell_j)\ &=e_i^*(I+\ell_1e_1^*)\cdots (I+\ell_{j-1}e_{j-1}^*)\ell_j+e_i^*e_j\ &=e_i^*\ell_j+e_i^*e_j. \end{aligned} The factors with index greater than j leave e_j unchanged. After the j-th factor produces e_j+\ell_j, the factors with index less than j leave both vectors unchanged, because their relevant coordinates are zero.The resulting matrix has ones on the diagonal and the multipliers \ell_{ij} below it. Therefore the lower-triangular factor in A=LU is obtained simply by storing these multipliers.3.5.3 Completing the Inversion PropertyFor a product of elementary factors, Theorem 3 and closure under multiplication show that its inverse is lower triangular: (L_m\cdots L_1)^{-1}=L_1^{-1}\cdots L_m^{-1}. For the general nonsingular lower-triangular matrix, the missing step can be supplied directly by forward substitution.Lecture 03, p. 5 discusses the inverse of a product of elementary unit-lower-triangular matrices. That argument alone does not cover arbitrary nonsingular lower-triangular matrices. The short forward-substitution argument is an editorial completion using the method already developed in Lecture 02; it is not additional material quoted from the sheet. Solve Lx=e_j. For i<j, the right-hand side is zero, and forward substitution gives x_1=\cdots=x_{j-1}=0. Since this solution is the j-th column of L^{-1}, every entry of L^{-1} above its diagonal is zero. This completes Theorem 2.3.6 Computing LU in PlaceGaussian elimination transforms A into U. The multipliers needed for L can be stored in the positions below the diagonal that elimination would otherwise set to zero.At step k,  egin{aligned} A_{ik}&\leftarrow rac{A_{ik}}{A_{kk}}, &&i=k+1,\ldots,m,\ A_{ij}&\leftarrow A_{ij}-A_{ik}A_{kj}, &&i,j=k+1,\ldots,m. \end{aligned} In the second line, A_{ik} is already the stored multiplier. The result overwrites A with both factors: A_{ij}^{	ext{stored}} = egin{cases} L_{ij},&i>j,\ U_{ij},&i\le j. \end{cases} \qquad L_{ii}=1. The diagonal of L does not need to be stored.3.6.1 Algorithm: LU Factorization without PivotingThe following is a Julia-style transcription of the algorithm on the sheet. The input array is assumed to be square and to have a floating-point element type. A zero-pivot check makes the algorithm’s required assumption explicit.Lecture 03, p. 6 uses a vector slice to divide the entries below a pivot. The scalar loop performs the same updates. The explicit zero-pivot guard is an editorial addition; it does not choose a new pivot or change the factorization algorithm.The trailing update uses i as the inner-loop index, consistent with the column-oriented memory traversal discussed in Lecture 02.3.7 Zero PivotsDoes every invertible matrix admit the preceding LU factorization without pivoting? No. Consider A= egin{pmatrix}0&1\1&1\end{pmatrix}. The matrix is invertible, but the first pivot is A_{11}=0, so the algorithm immediately attempts division by zero.[!theorem] Nonzero pivots and leading submatrices Suppose elimination without pivoting has completed its first k-1 steps with nonzero pivots. If the k-th pivot is zero, then the leading principal submatrix A[1\!:\!k,1\!:\!k] is singular.Consequently, elimination has nonzero pivots throughout if and only if every leading principal submatrix is nonsingular.3.7.1 ProofThe triangular structure gives the leading-block identity  egin{aligned} A[1\!:\!k,1\!:\!k] &=L[1\!:\!k,:]\,U[:,1\!:\!k]\ &=\underbrace{L[1\!:\!k,1\!:\!k]}_{	ext{unit lower triangular}} \underbrace{U[1\!:\!k,1\!:\!k]}_{	ext{upper triangular}}. \end{aligned} The leading block of L is nonsingular because its diagonal entries are one. The leading block of U is nonsingular exactly when its first k diagonal entries—the pivots—are nonzero.The same leading-block relation is available at step k before dividing by the k-th pivot. Given nonzero earlier pivots, a zero k-th pivot makes that leading block singular. Conversely, nonsingularity of each successive leading block prevents a zero pivot and allows elimination to continue.3.8 Small Pivots Can Also Be DangerousAvoiding an exactly zero pivot is not enough to ensure an accurate computation. The lecture perturbs the preceding example to A= egin{pmatrix}\epsilon&1\1&1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad 0<\epsilon\ll1. In this example, \epsilon is a small matrix entry. In the next section, the lecture reuses \epsilon for machine precision.The exact factors are L= egin{pmatrix}1&0\\epsilon^{-1}&1\end{pmatrix}, \qquad U= egin{pmatrix}\epsilon&1\0&1-\epsilon^{-1}\end{pmatrix}. Their exact product isLecture 03, p. 7 displays a minus sign between the two contributions to the lower-right product entry. The factors immediately above it require \epsilon^{-1}+(1-\epsilon^{-1}), not \epsilon^{-1}-(1-\epsilon^{-1}). The product is corrected explicitly.  egin{aligned} LU &= egin{pmatrix} \epsilon&1\ 1&\underbrace{\epsilon^{-1}+(1-\epsilon^{-1})}_{1} \end{pmatrix}\ &=A. \end{aligned} However, if \epsilon^{-1} is so large that the 1 is lost when evaluating 1-\epsilon^{-1}, while the intermediate numbers remain representable, then \operatorname{fl}(1-\epsilon^{-1})\approx-\epsilon^{-1}. The cancellation in the reconstructed product can then give LU\ 	ext{computed in floating point} \ \approx\  egin{pmatrix}\epsilon&1\1&0\end{pmatrix}, rather than  egin{pmatrix}\epsilon&1\1&1\end{pmatrix}. The arrow toward  egin{pmatrix}0&1\1&0\end{pmatrix} in the sheet is a schematic illustration of this floating-point failure for small \epsilon, not the exact-arithmetic limit of LU.The small pivot creates large multipliers and large intermediate entries whose cancellation can lose the desired result.3.9 Error Analysis: The Main EstimateThe lecture ends by stating an error estimate for the computed LU factors. Here \epsilon denotes machine precision, and absolute values of matrices are taken entrywise.[!theorem] Componentwise LU error estimate — first-order form Suppose LU factorization terminates without a zero pivot and the floating-point operations remain in the relative-error regime. For the computed factors L and U, write A=LU+E. To first order in machine precision, the lecture’s estimate is  oxed{|E|\lesssim m\epsilon\,|L|\,|U|.} The product |L|\,|U| is an ordinary matrix product of the entrywise absolute values, not the entrywise absolute value of LU.The handwritten sheet writes |E|\le m\epsilon|L||U|. It suppresses higher-order rounding factors; the first-order interpretation is made explicit here rather than treating the stated coefficient as an exact finite-precision bound.Lecture 03, p. 8 and Lecture 04, pp. 2–3. The derivation uses first-order rounding estimates. The transcribed statement is qualified accordingly; overflow and underflow outside a relative-error model are not covered.Lecture 04 develops this estimate and uses it to motivate pivoting.

1 Numerical Methods I, Homework 3Schäfer, Fall 2026Deadline: Oct. 19 Monday, 4:55 pmSubmit your answers as a PDF file (report the results that you obtain using programming by using plots, tables, and a description of your implementation like you would when writing a paper.) Also submit your code in a zip file.Programming questions are posted in Julia. You are allowed to use basic library functions like sorting, plotting, matrix-vector products etc, but nothing that renders the problem itself trivial. Please use your common sense and ask the instructors if you are unsure. You should never add additional packages to the environment.Homework is due at the beginning of class. Deadlines are sharp.We recommend the use of LaTeX for typing up your solutions. No credit will be given to unreadable handwriting.1 Schur Complements I [25 pts]Consider the matrix \mathbf{M} \coloneqq  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B}\ \mathbf{C} & \mathbf{D} \end{pmatrix}, 	ag{1} where \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{K}^{m 	imes m} and \mathbf{D} \in \mathbb{K}^{n 	imes n} are square matrices and \mathbf{A} is invertible. The matrix \mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A} \coloneqq \mathbf{D} - \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} is called the Schur complement of \mathbf{A} in \mathbf{M}.1.1.1 (a) [5 pts]Relate the Schur complement \mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A} to the block LU factorization of \mathbf{M}.1.1.1.1 Solution to 1(a)1.1.2 (b) [5 pts]Show that the determinant of \mathbf{M} is given by \det\left(\mathbf{A}ight) \det\left(\mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A}ight).1.1.2.1 Solution to 1(b)1.1.3 (c) [7.5 pts]Let \mathbf{M} = \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U} be the LU factorization of \mathbf{M}. Split its factors into block matrices \mathbf{L} =  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{L}_{1, 1} & \mathbf{0} \ \mathbf{L}_{2, 1} & \mathbf{L}_{2, 2} \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{U} =  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{U}_{1, 1} & \mathbf{U}_{1, 2} \ \mathbf{0} & \mathbf{U}_{2, 2} \end{pmatrix} 	ag{2} according to the same blocking as \mathbf{M}. Show that \mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{L}_{2, 2} \mathbf{U}_{2, 2}.1.1.3.1 Solution to 1(c)Multiplying the blocks of \mathbf{L}\mathbf{U} and comparing with \mathbf{M} gives  egin{aligned} \mathbf{A}&=\mathbf{L}_{1,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,1}\ \mathbf{B}&=\mathbf{L}_{1,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2}\ \mathbf{C}&=\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,1}\ \mathbf{D}&=\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2} +\mathbf{L}_{2,2}\mathbf{U}_{2,2} \end{aligned} Since we know that \mathbf{A} is invertible, its square factors \mathbf{L}_{1,1} and \mathbf{U}_{1,1} are also invertible. Therefore  egin{aligned} \mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B} &=(\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,1}) (\mathbf{U}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{L}_{1,1}^{-1}) (\mathbf{L}_{1,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2})\ &=\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2} \end{aligned} Subtracting this from \mathbf{D} give us that  egin{aligned} \mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A} &=\mathbf{D}-\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B}\ &=\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2} +\mathbf{L}_{2,2}\mathbf{U}_{2,2} -\mathbf{L}_{2,1}\mathbf{U}_{1,2}\ &= oxed{\mathbf{L}_{2,2}\mathbf{U}_{2,2}} \end{aligned} 1.1.4 (d) [7.5 pts]Now consider the 3 	imes 3 block matrix \mathbf{N} =  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{N}_{1, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{1, 2} & \mathbf{N}_{1, 3} \ \mathbf{N}_{2, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{2, 2} & \mathbf{N}_{2, 3} \ \mathbf{N}_{3, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{3, 2} & \mathbf{N}_{3, 3} \end{pmatrix}. 	ag{3} Prove the Quotient Property of Schur complements \left(\mathbf{N} / \mathbf{N}_{1, 1}ight) / \left(\mathbf{N} / \mathbf{N}_{1, 1}ight)_{1, 1} = \mathbf{N} \Big /  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{N}_{1, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{1, 2} \ \mathbf{N}_{2, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{2, 2} \end{pmatrix}. 	ag{4} Here, \left(\mathbf{N} / \mathbf{N}_{1, 1}ight)_{1, 1} is the top left block of \mathbf{N} / \mathbf{N}_{1, 1}, and the blocks \mathbf{N}_{1,1} and  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{N}_{1, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{1, 2} \ \mathbf{N}_{2, 1} & \mathbf{N}_{2, 2} \end{pmatrix} are assumed to be invertible.1.1.4.1 Solution to 1(d)Write \mathbf{S}\coloneqq\mathbf{N}/\mathbf{N}_{1,1} = egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{S}_{1,1}&\mathbf{S}_{1,2}\ \mathbf{S}_{2,1}&\mathbf{S}_{2,2} \end{pmatrix} where the four blocks are  egin{aligned} \mathbf{S}_{1,1} &=\mathbf{N}_{2,2}-\mathbf{N}_{2,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,2}\ \mathbf{S}_{1,2} &=\mathbf{N}_{2,3}-\mathbf{N}_{2,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,3}\ \mathbf{S}_{2,1} &=\mathbf{N}_{3,2}-\mathbf{N}_{3,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,2}\ \mathbf{S}_{2,2} &=\mathbf{N}_{3,3}-\mathbf{N}_{3,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,3} \end{aligned} Let \mathbf{K} denote the leading two-by-two block matrix on the right-hand side of (4). By part (b), \det(\mathbf{K}) =\det(\mathbf{N}_{1,1})\det(\mathbf{S}_{1,1}) The two assumed inverses therefore imply that \mathbf{S}_{1,1} is invertible.To evaluate the Schur complement with respect to \mathbf{K}, solve \mathbf{K}  egin{pmatrix}\mathbf{X}\\mathbf{Y}\end{pmatrix} = egin{pmatrix}\mathbf{N}_{1,3}\\mathbf{N}_{2,3}\end{pmatrix} Elimination in this block system gives  egin{aligned} \mathbf{X} &=\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1} (\mathbf{N}_{1,3}-\mathbf{N}_{1,2}\mathbf{Y})\ \mathbf{S}_{1,1}\mathbf{Y}&=\mathbf{S}_{1,2}\ \mathbf{Y}&=\mathbf{S}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{S}_{1,2} \end{aligned} Substitute these expressions into \mathbf{N}/\mathbf{K}:  egin{aligned} \mathbf{N}/\mathbf{K} &=\mathbf{N}_{3,3} -\mathbf{N}_{3,1}\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{N}_{3,2}\mathbf{Y}\ &=\mathbf{N}_{3,3} -\mathbf{N}_{3,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,3}\ &\quad- igl(\mathbf{N}_{3,2} -\mathbf{N}_{3,1}\mathbf{N}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{N}_{1,2} igr) \mathbf{S}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{S}_{1,2}\ &=\mathbf{S}_{2,2} -\mathbf{S}_{2,1}\mathbf{S}_{1,1}^{-1}\mathbf{S}_{1,2}\ &=\mathbf{S}/\mathbf{S}_{1,1} \end{aligned} Since \mathbf{S}=\mathbf{N}/\mathbf{N}_{1,1}, this is precisely the quotient property (4): successive elimination and elimination of the combined block give the same Schur complement.2 Schur Complements II [25 pts + 10 bonus]Let \mathbf{M} be as in the previous problem.1.2.1 (a) [5 pts]Divide the inverse of \mathbf{M} into blocks (of the same size as those of \mathbf{M}) as \mathbf{M}^{-1} =  egin{pmatrix}  oldsymbol{\alpha} &  oldsymbol{ eta} \  oldsymbol{\gamma} &  oldsymbol{\delta} \end{pmatrix}. 	ag{5} Show that the Schur complement \mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A} is the inverse of  oldsymbol{\delta}.1.2.1.1 Solution to 2(a)Write \mathbf{S}=\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}. Since \mathbf{M} and \mathbf{A} are invertible, Question 1(b) implies that \mathbf{S} is invertible. Invert the block LU factors from Question 1(a) in reverse order: \mathbf{M}^{-1} =  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{A}^{-1}&-\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{S}^{-1}\ \mathbf{0}&\mathbf{S}^{-1} \end{pmatrix}  egin{pmatrix} \mathbf{I}_m&\mathbf{0}\ -\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}^{-1}&\mathbf{I}_n \end{pmatrix} The lower-right block of this product is \mathbf{S}^{-1}. Comparing with (5),  oldsymbol{\delta}=\mathbf{S}^{-1} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  oxed{\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}= oldsymbol{\delta}^{-1}} 1.2.2 (b) [5 pts]For \mathbf{M} symmetric positive definite (symmetric meaning Hermitian in the complex case), show that if \lambda is an eigenvalue of \mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A}, then \lambda_{\min}\left(\mathbf{M} ight) \leq \lambda \leq \lambda_{\max}\left(\mathbf{M}ight).1.2.2.1 Solution to 2(b)Because \mathbf{M} is Hermitian positive definite, so is \mathbf{M}^{-1}. Its lower-right principal block  oldsymbol{\delta} is positive definite, since for  oldsymbol{y}
e oldsymbol{0},  oldsymbol{y}^* oldsymbol{\delta} oldsymbol{y} =  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{0}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}^* \mathbf{M}^{-1}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{0}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}>0 Thus \mathbf{S}=\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}= oldsymbol{\delta}^{-1} is also Hermitian positive definite.The eigendecomposition used in Lecture 8 gives, for every vector  oldsymbol{z},  rac{\| oldsymbol{z}\|_2^2}{\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{M})} \le  oldsymbol{z}^*\mathbf{M}^{-1} oldsymbol{z} \le  rac{\| oldsymbol{z}\|_2^2}{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M})} Indeed, in an orthonormal eigenbasis, the quadratic form is a sum of squared coordinate magnitudes weighted by the eigenvalues 1/\lambda_j(\mathbf{M}).Now take an eigenvector  oldsymbol{y}
e oldsymbol{0} with \mathbf{S} oldsymbol{y}=\lambda oldsymbol{y}. Then  oldsymbol{\delta} oldsymbol{y}=\lambda^{-1} oldsymbol{y}. Applying the preceding bound to  oldsymbol{z}=( oldsymbol{0}, oldsymbol{y})^T yields  rac{\| oldsymbol{y}\|_2^2}{\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{M})} \le  rac{\| oldsymbol{y}\|_2^2}{\lambda} \le  rac{\| oldsymbol{y}\|_2^2}{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M})} Divide by \| oldsymbol{y}\|_2^2 and take reciprocals, reversing the inequalities:  oxed{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M}) \le\lambda\le\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{M})} 1.2.3 (c) [7.5 pts]Show that the size of the pivots occurring in the Cholesky or LU factorization of a s.p.d. matrix \mathbf{M} is lower bounded by \left\|\mathbf{M}^{-1}ight\|^{-1}, where \|\cdot\| denotes the spectral norm.1.2.3.1 Solution to 2(c)As in Lecture 9, a pivot is the diagonal entry of the active matrix before taking a square root. After eliminating the first k-1 variables, the active matrix is \mathbf{S}^{(k-1)} =\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{M}_{1:k-1,\,1:k-1} \qquad (k\ge2) The next pivot is p_k=(\mathbf{S}^{(k-1)})_{1,1}. The leading principal block is positive definite, so this Schur complement exists. By part (b) and the quadratic-form bound,  egin{aligned} p_k &= oldsymbol{e}_1^*\mathbf{S}^{(k-1)} oldsymbol{e}_1\ &\ge\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{S}^{(k-1)})\ &\ge\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M}) \end{aligned} For the first pivot, the same bound follows directly from p_1= oldsymbol{e}_1^*\mathbf{M} oldsymbol{e}_1. Since \mathbf{M} is positive definite, \|\mathbf{M}^{-1}\|_2 = rac{1}{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M})} Therefore every elimination pivot satisfies  oxed{p_k\ge\|\mathbf{M}^{-1}\|_2^{-1}} In unit-lower-triangular LU, p_k=U_{k,k}. In Cholesky, p_k=L_{k,k}^2, so the factor’s positive diagonal entries satisfy L_{k,k}\ge\|\mathbf{M}^{-1}\|_2^{-1/2}.1.2.4 (d) [7.5 pts]For \mathbf{M} s.p.d, show that  oldsymbol{y}^* \left(\mathbf{M} / \mathbf{A}ight)  oldsymbol{y} = \min \limits_{ oldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{K}^{m}}  egin{pmatrix}  oldsymbol{x}\  oldsymbol{y} \end{pmatrix}^* \mathbf{M}  egin{pmatrix}  oldsymbol{x}\  oldsymbol{y} \end{pmatrix}. 	ag{6} 1.2.4.1 Solution to 2(d)Hermitian symmetry gives \mathbf{C}=\mathbf{B}^* and (\mathbf{A}^{-1})^*=\mathbf{A}^{-1}. Expand and complete the square:  egin{aligned}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{x}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}^* \mathbf{M}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{x}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix} &= oldsymbol{x}^*\mathbf{A} oldsymbol{x} + oldsymbol{x}^*\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y} + oldsymbol{y}^*\mathbf{B}^* oldsymbol{x} + oldsymbol{y}^*\mathbf{D} oldsymbol{y}\ &=( oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y})^* \mathbf{A} ( oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y})\ &\quad+ oldsymbol{y}^* (\mathbf{D}-\mathbf{B}^*\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B})  oldsymbol{y} \end{aligned} Since \mathbf{A} is positive definite, the first term is nonnegative and vanishes exactly at  oldsymbol{x}=-\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y} The second term is  oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}) oldsymbol{y}. Consequently,  oxed{ \min_{ oldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{K}^m}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{x}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}^* \mathbf{M}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{x}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix} = oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}) oldsymbol{y} } 1.2.5 (e) [10 bonus pts]By replacing the \min with a \min \max over suitable variables, derive a version of the results of (d) that is valid for general \mathbf{M}.1.2.5.1 Solution to 2(e)Introduce an independent vector  oldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{K}^m in the left argument and define  egin{aligned} F( oldsymbol{x}, oldsymbol{z}) &= egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{z}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}^* \mathbf{M}  egin{pmatrix} oldsymbol{x}\ oldsymbol{y}\end{pmatrix}\ &= oldsymbol{z}^*(\mathbf{A} oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y}) + oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{C} oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{D} oldsymbol{y}) \end{aligned} First let \mathbb{K}=\mathbb{R}. For fixed  oldsymbol{x}, write  oldsymbol{r}=\mathbf{A} oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y}. If  oldsymbol{r}
e oldsymbol{0}, choosing  oldsymbol{z}=t oldsymbol{r} and letting t	o+\infty makes F tend to +\infty. If  oldsymbol{r}= oldsymbol{0}, the expression is independent of  oldsymbol{z}. Thus \sup_{ oldsymbol{z}}F( oldsymbol{x}, oldsymbol{z}) = egin{cases}  oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{C} oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{D} oldsymbol{y}) &\mathbf{A} oldsymbol{x}+\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y}= oldsymbol{0}\ +\infty&	ext{otherwise} \end{cases} Since \mathbf{A} is invertible, the only finite case is  oldsymbol{x}=-\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{B} oldsymbol{y}. Substitution gives the general real-matrix version:  oxed{  oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}) oldsymbol{y} =\min_{ oldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^m} \sup_{ oldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{R}^m} F( oldsymbol{x}, oldsymbol{z}) } The supremum allows the value +\infty away from the constraint; at the minimizing  oldsymbol{x}, every  oldsymbol{z} attains the inner maximum. Neither symmetry nor positive definiteness is required.For a general complex matrix, the scalar on the left may be complex, so apply minimization and maximization to its real and imaginary parts:  egin{aligned} \operatorname{Re}\!\left( oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}) oldsymbol{y}ight) &=\min_{ oldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{C}^m} \sup_{ oldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{C}^m} \operatorname{Re}F( oldsymbol{x}, oldsymbol{z})\ \operatorname{Im}\!\left( oldsymbol{y}^*(\mathbf{M}/\mathbf{A}) oldsymbol{y}ight) &=\min_{ oldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{C}^m} \sup_{ oldsymbol{z}\in\mathbb{C}^m} \operatorname{Im}F( oldsymbol{x}, oldsymbol{z}) \end{aligned} The same proof uses  oldsymbol{z}=t oldsymbol{r} for the real part and  oldsymbol{z}=-it oldsymbol{r} for the imaginary part. For Hermitian \mathbf{M}, the Schur-complement quadratic form is real, so the first identity alone gives its value.3 Cholesky and QR [25 pts]1.3.1 (a) [5 pts]Assume that the invertible matrix \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{K}^{m 	imes m} satisfies \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}. Derive a way to write \mathbf{A} as the sum of rank-one matrices in outer