1 Problem 1: Finite-Difference SolutionWe wish to discretize and solve the boundary-value problem-\mu rac{d^2u}{dx^2} +  eta rac{du}{dx} = f(x),\quad x\in(0,1),\quad u(0)=u(1)=0Or equivalently: egin{cases} -\mu u''(x) +  eta u'(x) = f(x), & 0<x<1,\ u(0)=0,\; u(1)=0. \end{cases}Where \mu>0 and  eta is a constant (possibly negative), and f\in C^0(0,1).1. Finite-Difference DiscretizationDivide [0,1] into N equal subintervals so that \Delta x =  rac{1}{N}. Letx_j = j\Delta x,\quad j=0,1,2,\dots,N,so that x_0=0 and x_N=1. We approximate u(x_j)\approx u_j. The boundary conditions become u_0=0 and u_N=0.1.1 Central Difference Scheme (for the interior points)A standard centered second-difference for u''(x) at x_j is:u''(x_j) \approx  rac{u_{j+1} - 2u_j + u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2}.A standard centered first-difference for u'(x) at x_j is:u'(x_j) \approx  rac{u_{j+1} - u_{j-1}}{2\Delta x}.Hence, the PDE -\mu u''(x) +  eta u'(x)=f(x) becomes for j=1,\dots,N-1:-\mu rac{u_{j+1} - 2u_j + u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2} +  eta rac{u_{j+1} - u_{j-1}}{2\Delta x} = f(x_j).We impose u_0 = 0 and u_N=0.2. Local Truncation ErrorThe second-order central difference for u'' is O((\Delta x)^2) accurate.The central difference for u' is also O((\Delta x)^2) accurate.Hence the local truncation error of the combined scheme is O((\Delta x)^2).3. Matrix FormCollect unknowns u_1,u_2,\dots,u_{N-1} into a vector \mathbf{u}=(u_1,\dots,u_{N-1})^T. The boundary values u_0=0 and u_N=0 are known.Rewrite the finite-difference equation for an interior index j:-\mu rac{u_{j+1} - 2u_j + u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2} +  eta rac{u_{j+1} - u_{j-1}}{2\Delta x} = f(x_j).We can factor out coefficients:Let \alpha \equiv  rac{\mu}{(\Delta x)^2}.Let \gamma \equiv  rac{ eta}{2\Delta x}.Then the coefficient of u_j is 2\alpha, the coefficient of u_{j+1} is -\alpha + \gamma, and the coefficient of u_{j-1} is -\alpha - \gamma. Thus, in matrix form: egin{pmatrix} 2\alpha & -\alpha+\gamma & 0 & \cdots & 0\ -\alpha-\gamma & 2\alpha & -\alpha+\gamma & \cdots & 0\ 0 & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots & \vdots\ \vdots & & -\alpha-\gamma & 2\alpha & -\alpha+\gamma\ 0 & \cdots & 0 & -\alpha-\gamma & 2\alpha \end{pmatrix}  egin{pmatrix} u_1\ u_2\ \vdots \ u_{N-2}\ u_{N-1} \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} f(x_1)\ f(x_2)\ \vdots \ f(x_{N-2})\ f(x_{N-1}) \end{pmatrix}.This is a tridiagonal linear system, solvable by standard methods (e.g., Thomas algorithm).4. Quality of the Solution vs.  eta/\muThe ratio  rac{ eta}{\mu} often plays the role of a Péclet-type number in advection-diffusion problems.If  rac{ eta}{\mu} is small (diffusion-dominated), the solution is usually smooth and well-behaved under central differencing.If  rac{ eta}{\mu} is large (advection-dominated), pure central differences may produce spurious oscillations unless \Delta x is refined or upwinding techniques are used to stabilize the discrete solution.5. Upwind Method (First Order) for  eta<0When  eta<0, the flow is from right to left, so an upwind difference for the first derivative  eta u'(x) uses values on the right side at each j. Concretely, for  eta<0, we replace:u'(x_j) \approx  rac{u_{j+1} - u_j}{\Delta x} \quad 	ext{(a "backward" upwind if flow is leftward)}.Hence, the difference equation becomes:-\mu rac{u_{j+1} - 2u_j + u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2} +  eta rac{u_{j+1} - u_j}{\Delta x} = f(x_j), \quad j=1,\dots,N-1.(This replaces the central difference in the advective term by a one-sided upwind difference.)5.1 No Spurious OscillationsThe first-order upwind scheme for linear advection-diffusion is known to be monotone for any \Delta x>0 when  eta<0 (or, more generally, for any sign of  eta if we choose the correct upwind direction). Monotonicity prevents nonphysical oscillations. In short:Central difference can oscillate if | eta| is large relative to \mu.Upwind difference sacrifices some accuracy (only first order in \Delta x for the advective term) but remains stable and nonoscillatory for any step size \Delta x.Thus, with  eta<0, the upwind approach u'(x_j)\approx (u_{j+1}-u_j)/\Delta x ensures a stable, physically plausible solution without oscillations.6. SummaryEquation & Discretization -\mu u'' +  eta u' = f(x), \quad u(0)=u(1)=0 \longrightarrow  egin{cases} -\mu rac{u_{j+1}-2u_j+u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2} + eta rac{u_{j+1}-u_{j-1}}{2\Delta x} = f(x_j),\ u_0=0,\;u_N=0. \end{cases}Local Truncation Error is O((\Delta x)^2) for the centered scheme.Matrix Form: A standard tridiagonal system with bands -\alpha\mp \gamma, 2\alpha, -\alpha\pm \gamma.Effect of  eta/\mu: If | eta| is large relative to \mu, central differences can produce oscillatory solutions; upwind methods help.Upwind Method (for  eta<0): -\mu rac{u_{j+1}-2u_j+u_{j-1}}{(\Delta x)^2} +  eta rac{u_{j+1}-u_j}{\Delta x} = f(x_j) prevents oscillations for any \Delta x.2 Problem 2Solution OutlineWe have the linear advection equation rac{\partial u}{\partial t} \;+\; a\, rac{\partial u}{\partial x} \;=\; 0,\quad x\in \mathbb{R},\;t>0, with initial condition u(x,0) = u_0(x). We wish to:Derive the Lax–Wendroff scheme for this PDE.Determine the CFL condition for stability, i.e. find the condition on \displaystyle  rac{|a|\Delta t}{\Delta x}.2.1 1) Derivation of the Lax–Wendroff SchemeA succinct way to derive Lax–Wendroff is via a second-order Taylor expansion in time about t^n:u^{n+1}_i \;\approx\; u(x_i,\,t_n + \Delta t) \;=\; u(x_i,t_n) \;+\;\Delta t\, rac{\partial u}{\partial t} \;+\;	frac{(\Delta t)^2}{2}\, rac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2}\; igg|_{(x_i,t_n)}.From the PDE \partial_t u = -\,a\,\partial_x u, we can replace time-derivatives by spatial derivatives:First derivative in time:  rac{\partial u}{\partial t} \;=\; -\,a\, rac{\partial u}{\partial x}.Second derivative in time:  rac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} \;=\;  rac{\partial}{\partial t}\Bigl(-\,a\, rac{\partial u}{\partial x}\Bigr) \;=\; -\,a\, rac{\partial}{\partial x}\Bigl( rac{\partial u}{\partial t}\Bigr) \;=\; -\,a\, rac{\partial}{\partial x}\Bigl(-\,a\, rac{\partial u}{\partial x}\Bigr) \;=\; 