Goal of this chapter. Use the geodesic equation in Schwarzschild spacetime to derive conserved quantities, the radial “energy” equation with an effective potential V(r), circular‑orbit conditions (including the ISCO), and simple astrophysical implications. All formulas preserve Moore’s notation; each result is derived in one continuous aligned environment. Underbraces are used only for derivation clarifications.10.1 Geodesic equation (working form)We start from the lowered‑index geodesic equation (from Ch. 8), written component‑wise as  oxed{\ 0 \,=\,  rac{d}{d	au}\!\Big(g_{\mu
u}\, rac{dx^{
u}}{d	au}\Big)\;- 	frac12\,\partial_{\mu}g_{\alpha eta}\, rac{dx^{\alpha}}{d	au}\, rac{dx^{ eta}}{d	au}\ }	ag{10.1} This form is convenient in Schwarzschild coordinates because g_{\mu
u} is diagonal and depends only on r.10.2 Schwarzschild metric and componentsMetric (GR units):  oxed{\ ds^2 = -\Big(1-	frac{2GM}{r}\Big)dt^2 + \Big(1-	frac{2GM}{r}\Big)^{-1}\!dr^2 + r^2 d	heta^2 + r^2\sin^2\!	heta\,d\phi^2 }	ag{10.2} Read off the nonzero components:  oxed{\ g_{tt}=-(1-	frac{2GM}{r}),\quad g_{rr}=(1-	frac{2GM}{r})^{-1},\quad g_{	heta	heta}=r^2,\quad g_{\phi\phi}=r^2\sin^2\!	heta }	ag{10.3}10.3 Conserved quantities from symmetries3.1 (a) Time‑translation symmetry → energy per unit mass e egin{aligned} 0&= rac{d}{d	au}\!\Big(g_{t
u}\, rac{dx^{
u}}{d	au}\Big)-	frac12\,\partial_{t}g_{\alpha eta}\, rac{dx^{\alpha}}{d	au}\, rac{dx^{ eta}}{d	au} \ &= rac{d}{d	au}\!\Big(g_{tt}\, rac{dt}{d	au}\Big)\;\underbrace{-\;0}_{\partial_t g_{\alpha eta}=0}\;\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ g_{tt}\, rac{dt}{d	au}=	ext{const}\equiv -e }\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ e=(1-	frac{2GM}{r})\, rac{dt}{d	au} } \end{aligned} 	ag{10.5} The constant e is the relativistic energy per unit mass at infinity (since at r\!	o\!\infty, e	o u^t).3.2 (b) Rotational symmetry about z‑axis → angular momentum per unit mass Q egin{aligned} 0&= rac{d}{d	au}\!\Big(g_{\phi
u}\, rac{dx^{
u}}{d	au}\Big)-	frac12\,\partial_{\phi}g_{\alpha eta}\, rac{dx^{\alpha}}{d	au}\, rac{dx^{ eta}}{d	au} \ &= rac{d}{d	au}\!\Big(g_{\phi\phi}\, rac{d\phi}{d	au}\Big)\;\underbrace{-\;0}_{\partial_\phi g_{\alpha eta}=0}\;\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ g_{\phi\phi}\, rac{d\phi}{d	au}=	ext{const}\equiv Q }\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ Q=r^2\sin^2\!	heta\, rac{d\phi}{d	au} }	ag{10.7} \end{aligned} Every geodesic lies in a plane through the origin; set 	heta=\pi/2 (equatorial plane) so Q=r^2\,d\phi/d	au.10.4 Radial “energy” equation and effective potentialUse normalization u\!\cdot\!u=-1 on the equatorial plane (	heta=\pi/2, d	heta=0):  egin{aligned} -1&=g_{tt}\Big( rac{dt}{d	au}\Big)^2+g_{rr}\Big( rac{dr}{d	au}\Big)^2+g_{\phi\phi}\Big( rac{d\phi}{d	au}\Big)^2\[2pt] &= -\, rac{e^2}{\,1- rac{2GM}{r}\,}\;\underbrace{+\;\Big(1-	frac{2GM}{r}\Big)^{-1}\Big( rac{dr}{d	au}\Big)^2}_{g_{rr}(dr/d	au)^2}\;+\; rac{Q^2}{r^2}\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\;\Big( rac{dr}{d	au}\Big)^2\;=\;e^2-\Big(1-	frac{2GM}{r}\Big)\Big(1+ rac{Q^2}{r^2}\Big) \[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ 	frac12\Big( rac{dr}{d	au}\Big)^2\;=\;E\;- \underbrace{\Big[\, rac{GM}{r}+ rac{Q^2}{2r^2}- rac{GMQ^2}{r^3}\,\Big]}_{\displaystyle V(r)}\ }	ag{10.8–10.9} \end{aligned} where E\equiv 	frac12(e^2-1). This has the conservation form E=K+V(r) with “radial kinetic energy per unit mass” K=	frac12 (dr/d	au)^2.Newtonian analogue. With Newtonian time t and Q=r^2\,d\phi/dt, the energy equation is  oxed{\ 	frac12\Big( rac{dr}{dt}\Big)^2 + V_N(r) = E_N,\qquad V_N(r) = - rac{GM}{r}+ rac{Q^2}{2r^2} }	ag{10.10} The only formal difference is the extra -GMQ^2/r^3 term in V(r) for Schwarzschild.10.5 Circular orbits and Kepler’s third law5.1 (a) Circular‑orbit radii from dV/dr=0 egin{aligned} 0&= rac{dV}{dr}= rac{GM}{r^2}- rac{Q^2}{r^3}+ rac{3GMQ^2}{r^4} \;\Rightarrow\; GM r^2 - Q^2 r + 3GMQ^2=0\ &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ r_c= rac{Q^2}{2GM}\Big(1\pm\sqrt{1-12\,(GM/Q)^2}\Big) }	ag{10.11} \end{aligned} The smaller root is unstable, the larger stable.5.2 (b) Kepler’s third law (period as measured at infinity)For a circular orbit, dr/d	au=0. Using Q=r^2\,d\phi/d	au and e=(1-2GM/r)\,dt/d	au, one finds the angular speed seen at infinity \Omega\equiv d\phi/dt=Q/(e\,r^2). Eliminating e via the circular‑orbit condition yields  oxed{\ \Omega^2= rac{GM}{r_c^3}\ \;\Longleftrightarrow\;\ T^2= rac{4\pi^2}{GM}\,r_c^3 }	ag{10.12} This is Kepler’s law with the period T measured by an observer at infinity.10.6 Radial acceleration equations (model‑friendly form)Differentiate the energy equations w.r.t. their parameters:  egin{aligned} 	ext{Schwarzschild: }\quad 0&= rac{d}{d	au}\Big[	frac12\Big( rac{dr}{d	au}\Big)^2+V(r)\Big] = rac{dr}{d	au}\, rac{d^2 r}{d	au^2}+ rac{dV}{dr}\, rac{dr}{d	au}\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\  rac{d^2 r}{d	au^2}= -\, rac{GM}{r^2}+ rac{Q^2}{r^3}- rac{3GMQ^2}{r^4} }	ag{10.13} \end{aligned}  oxed{\ 	ext{Newtonian:}\quad  rac{d^2 r}{dt^2}= -\, rac{GM}{r^2}+ rac{Q^2}{r^3} }	ag{10.14} For pure radial motion, Q=0, giving d^2 r/d	au^2= -GM/r^2 (same radial field law).10.7 Stability and the innermost stable circular orbit (ISCO)Stability requires d^2 V/dr^2 ig|_{r_c}>0. Applying this to (10.11) gives the condition  oxed{\ Q^2>12\,G^2M^2\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r_c>6GM }	ag{10.6*} The equality defines the ISCO at r_{	ext{ISCO}}=6GM. Below this, no stable circular orbits exist in Schwarzschild spacetime.10.8 Energy at the ISCO and accretion efficiencyFor a circular orbit (equatorial), combine normalization with the constants e,Q:  egin{aligned} -1&=g_{tt}\Big( rac{dt}{d	au}\Big)^2+g_{\phi\phi}\Big( rac{d\phi}{d	au}\Big)^2 = -\, rac{e^2}{1- rac{2GM}{r}}+ rac{Q^2}{r^2}\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ e^2=\Big(1-	frac{2GM}{r}\Big)\Big(1+ rac{Q^2}{r^2}\Big) }	ag{10.15} \end{aligned} At the ISCO, r=6GM and Q^2=12G^2M^2. Hence  egin{aligned} e^2&=\Big(1-	frac{1}{3}\Big)\Big(1+	frac{1}{3}\Big)=	frac{8}{9}\;\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ e=\sqrt{	frac{8}{9}}\approx0.943 }\[2pt] &\Rightarrow\; oxed{\ \Delta e \equiv 1-e \approx 0.057 }	ag{10.16} \end{aligned} Thus, matter spiraling in from infinity to the ISCO radiates \sim5.7\% of its rest‑mass energy before plunging.10.9 Disk luminosity and characteristic temperatureAssuming the inner annulus [R,2R] dominates and radiates as a blackbody:  oxed{\ L=\sigma\,A\,T^4 }\quad (\sigma=5.67	imes10^{-8}\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-4}})	ag{10.17} Since A\propto R^2 and R\sim r_{	ext{ISCO}}\propto GM, one finds  oxed{\ T\;\propto\;L^{1/4}\,M^{-1/2}\;\approx\;10^{6}\,\mathrm{K}\;\Big(	frac{L}{L_\odot}\Big)^{1/4}\Big(	frac{M}{M_\odot}\Big)^{-1/2} }	ag{10.18} Hence stellar‑mass compact objects with stellar‑like luminosities have X‑ray inner‑disk temperatures (photon energies \sim keV).10 Concept summaryGeodesic first integrals: e=(1-2GM/r)\,dt/d	au and Q=r^2 d\phi/d	au (10.5, 10.7).Radial energy equation: 	frac12(dr/d	au)^2=E-V(r) with V(r)=GM/r+Q^2/(2r^2)-GMQ^2/r^3 (10.8–10.9).Circular orbits: roots (10.11); Kepler law at infinity (10.12).Acceleration form for integration (10.13) vs Newtonian (10.14).Stability ⇒ ISCO at 6GM; ISCO energy and \Delta e\approx0.057 (10.15–10.16).Disk blackbody estimate and T scaling (10.17–10.18).

