5.1.1 Magnetic FieldsThe basic problem of classical electrodynamics is calculating the force that a collection of source charges (q_1, q_2, \dots) exerts on a test charge Q. While Electrostatics deals with source charges at rest, Magnetostatics addresses the forces between charges in motion.[!definition] The Origin of Magnetic Fields A stationary charge produces only an electric field \mathbf{E} in the space around it. However, a moving charge generates, in addition, a magnetic field \mathbf{B}.5.1.1.1 The Demonstration: Parallel and Antiparallel CurrentsTo illustrate the unique nature of magnetic forces, consider two parallel wires hanging from a ceiling. The force between them depends on the relative direction of the currents:Current ConfigurationPhysical ResultInteraction TypeOpposite DirectionsThe wires jump apart (Fig. 5.1a).RepulsionSame DirectionThe wires move together (Fig. 5.1b).Attraction[!Remark] Crucial Concept: Electrical Neutrality This force is not electrostatic in nature. The wires are electrically neutral because there are just as many stationary positive charges (atomic nuclei) as there are moving negative charges (electrons) in any given segment. A stationary test charge Q held near these wires would feel no force. The magnetic force only acts on charges that are also in motion.5.1.1.2 The Geometry of the B-FieldUnlike the electric field of a point charge (which points radially toward or away from the source), the magnetic field of a wire circles around it (Fig. 5.2). |673x717The Right-Hand Rule (RHR) for Fields: To determine the direction of the field:Point your right thumb in the direction of the current I.Your fingers will curl around the wire in the direction of the magnetic field \mathbf{B}.5.1.1.3 The Direction of Magnetic ForceThe direction of the resulting force \mathbf{F} is often counterintuitive. For two parallel wires carrying current in the same direction (upward):Field Generation: Wire 1 creates a magnetic field \mathbf{B} that points into the page at the location of Wire 2.Force Result: Despite the current being upward and the field being into the page, the resulting force \mathbf{F} on Wire 2 points to the left (toward Wire 1), as seen in Fig. 5.3.[!remark] Detecting Magnetic Fields In practice, magnetic fields are easily detected using a compass. The needle aligns itself with the local magnetic field \mathbf{B}. While it ordinarily points North due to the Earth’s field, laboratory fields are often strong enough to dominate the compass response.5.1.1.4 Trajectories in a Uniform Magnetic FieldDepending on the entry angle of the particle, the magnetic force creates distinct geometric paths.1. Cyclotron Motion (\mathbf{v} \perp \mathbf{B})If the velocity is perpendicular to the field, the magnetic force acts as a constant centripetal force, resulting in circular motion.[Image of cyclotron motion in a uniform magnetic field]Cyclotron Radius and Frequency Equating the magnetic force to the centripetal force (QvB =  rac{mv^2}{R}),5.1.2 Magnetic ForcesIn this section, we transition from the qualitative observations of wires and compasses to the formal mathematical law of the land for magnetic interactions. While the electric force is a simple push or pull, the magnetic force is inherently linked to velocity and three-dimensional geometry.5.1.2.1 The Lorentz Force LawThe magnetic force \mathbf{F}_{	ext{mag}} on a charge Q, moving with velocity \mathbf{v} in a magnetic field \mathbf{B}, is defined by the vector cross product.[!definition] Lorentz Force Law The total force on a charge Q in the presence of both electric and magnetic fields is:  oxed{\mathbf{F} = Q[\mathbf{E} + (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B})]}The Cross Product: The force is always perpendicular to both the velocity \mathbf{v} and the magnetic field \mathbf{B}.Stationary Charges: If \mathbf{v} = 0, the magnetic part of the force vanishes. Magnetic fields ignore stationary charges.Sign of Charge: For a negative charge (like an electron), the force direction is exactly opposite to the result of the standard Right-Hand Rule (use the back of your hand).5.1.2.2 Magnetic Forces Do No WorkThis is the most famous conceptual trap in magnetostatics. Because the magnetic force is always perpendicular to the direction of motion, it can change a particle’s direction, but never its speed or kinetic energy.[!definition] Magnetic Work Magnetic forces may alter the direction in which a particle moves, but they cannot speed it up or slow it down. The work done is:  oxed{dW_{	ext{mag}} = 0}Mathematical Proof:Work is defined as dW = \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{l}.Displacement d\mathbf{l} can be written as \mathbf{v} dt.Substitute the magnetic force: dW_{	ext{mag}} = Q(\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) \cdot \mathbf{v} dt.Since (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) is perpendicular to \mathbf{v} by definition of the cross product, their dot product is zero.5.1.3 Currents1. Definition of Current (I)Current is defined as the charge per unit time passing a given point in a wire.Units: Measured in Amperes (A), where 1	ext{ A} = 1	ext{ C/s}.Sign Convention: Positive charges moving to the right count the same as negative charges moving to the left. In practice, electrons (negative) move opposite to the direction of the electric current I.Line Charge Current: If a line charge with density \lambda travels down a wire at speed v, the current I is:[!definition] Electric Current (I) The current I in a wire is the charge per unit time passing a given point. It is measured in Amperes (A).1	ext{ A} = 1	ext{ C/s} For a line charge density \lambda moving at velocity \mathbf{v}, the current is:\mathbf{I} = \lambda \mathbf{v}2. Magnetic Force on a Line CurrentTo find the force on a segment of wire, we integrate the Lorentz force over all the charge elements in that segment.Derivation of the Force on a Wire:  egin{aligned} \mathbf{F}_{	ext{mag}} &= \int (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) dq && 	ext{\scriptsize Lorentz force law} \ &= \int (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) \lambda dl && \scriptsize dq = \lambda dl 	ext{ for line charges} \ &= \int (\underbrace{\lambda \mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{I}} 	imes \mathbf{B}) dl && \ &= \int I (d\mathbf{l} 	imes \mathbf{B}) && \scriptsize	ext{ since } \mathbf{I} dl = I d\mathbf{l} 	ext{ (Eq. 5.16)} \ &= I \int (d\mathbf{l} 	imes \mathbf{B}) && \end{aligned}[!definition] Force on a Wire For a wire carrying a constant current I, the total magnetic force is: oxed{\mathbf{F}_{	ext{mag}} = I \int (d\mathbf{l} 	imes \mathbf{B})}3. Surface Current Density (\mathbf{K})When charge flows over a surface (like a sheet of metal), we use the surface current density \mathbf{K}.Definition: The current per unit width-perpendicular-to-flow (dl_{\perp}). \mathbf{K} \equiv  rac{d\mathbf{I}}{dl_{\perp}}Relation to Velocity: If \sigma is the surface charge density, then  oxed{\mathbf{K} = \sigma \mathbf{v}}.Magnetic Force on a Surface Current:  egin{aligned} \mathbf{F}_{	ext{mag}} &= \int (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) \sigma da && 	ext{\scriptsize force on charge element } \sigma da \ &= \int (\underbrace{\sigma \mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{K}} 	imes \mathbf{B}) da \ &= \int (\mathbf{K} 	imes \mathbf{B}) da && 	ext{\scriptsize final integral over the surface area (Eq. 5.24)} \end{aligned}4. Volume Current Density (\mathbf{J})When charge is distributed throughout a 3D region, we use the volume current density \mathbf{J}.Definition: The current per unit area-perpendicular-to-flow (da_{\perp}). \mathbf{J} \equiv  rac{d\mathbf{I}}{da_{\perp}}Relation to Velocity: If ho is the volume charge density, then  oxed{\mathbf{J} = ho \mathbf{v}}.Magnetic Force on a Volume Current:  egin{aligned} \mathbf{F}_{	ext{mag}} &= \int (\mathbf{v} 	imes \mathbf{B}) ho d	au && 	ext{\scriptsize force on charge element } ho d	au \ &= \int (\underbrace{ho \mathbf{v}}_{\mathbf{J}} 	imes \mathbf{B}) d	au \ &= \int (\mathbf{J} 	imes \mathbf{B}) d	au && 	ext{\scriptsize final integral over the volume (Eq. 5.27)} \end{aligned}5. The Continuity EquationThis equation is the mathematical statement of Local Charge Conservation. It states that if charge is leaving a volume (outward flow of \mathbf{J}), the charge density ho inside must be decreasing.Derivation of the Continuity Equation:  egin{aligned} \oint_{\mathcal{S}} \mathbf{J} \cdot d\mathbf{a} &= \int_{\mathcal{V}} (
abla \cdot \mathbf{J}) d	au && 	ext{\scriptsize applying the Divergence Theorem} \ &= - rac{d}{dt} Q_{	ext{enc}} && 	ext{\scriptsize conservation: flow out = decrease in charge} \ &= - rac{d}{dt} \int_{\mathcal{V}} ho d	au && 	ext{\scriptsize expressing enclosed charge via density } ho \ \int_{\mathcal{V}} (
abla \cdot \mathbf{J}) d	au &= \int_{\mathcal{V}} \left( - rac{\partial ho}{\partial t} ight) d	au && 	ext{\scriptsize equating the two volume integrals} \end{aligned}Since this must hold for any volume, we get the Continuity Equation (Eq. 5.29):  oxed{
