9.3.1 Big pictureIn vacuum, Maxwell’s equations gave us waves traveling at speed c. In matter, the fields interact with the medium through polarization and magnetization, so the wave still propagates, but with a modified speed v= rac{1}{\sqrt{\epsilon\mu}}= rac{c}{n}. The main goals of this section are:derive wave propagation in a linear homogeneous medium,use electromagnetic boundary conditions to determine what happens at an interface,recover the laws of reflection and refraction,derive amplitude and energy relations for reflected and transmitted waves,understand the special role of polarization, including Brewster’s angle.9.3.1 Propagation in Linear Media9.3.1.1 Maxwell equations in matterInside matter, but in regions with no free charge and no free current, Maxwell’s equations are  egin{aligned} 
abla\cdot \mathbf D &= 0, \ 
abla\cdot \mathbf B &= 0, \ 
abla	imes \mathbf E &= - rac{\partial \mathbf B}{\partial t}, \ 
abla	imes \mathbf H &=  rac{\partial \mathbf D}{\partial t}. \end{aligned} If the medium is linear, \mathbf D=\epsilon \mathbf E, \qquad \mathbf H= rac{1}{\mu}\mathbf B, and homogeneous so that \epsilon and \mu are constants, then these become  egin{aligned} 
abla\cdot \mathbf E &= 0, \ 
abla\cdot \mathbf B &= 0, \ 
abla	imes \mathbf E &= - rac{\partial \mathbf B}{\partial t}, \ 
abla	imes \mathbf B &= \mu\epsilon  rac{\partial \mathbf E}{\partial t}. \end{aligned} These are exactly the vacuum equations with the replacement \mu_0\epsilon_0 \longrightarrow \mu\epsilon. So all the vacuum-wave machinery carries over with this substitution.9.3.1.2 Wave speed and index of refractionFrom the same curl-of-curl derivation used in vacuum,  oxed{ 
abla^2\mathbf E=\mu\epsilon rac{\partial^2 \mathbf E}{\partial t^2}, \qquad 
abla^2\mathbf B=\mu\epsilon rac{\partial^2 \mathbf B}{\partial t^2}} so the wave speed is  oxed{ v= rac{1}{\sqrt{\epsilon\mu}}= rac{c}{n}} with index of refraction  oxed{ n\equiv \sqrt{ rac{\epsilon\mu}{\epsilon_0\mu_0}} }. For most media, \mu\approx \mu_0, so  oxed{n\approx \sqrt{\epsilon_r}}. That is why light usually travels more slowly in matter than in vacuum.9.3.1.3 Plane-wave relations in matterAll the plane-wave structure from vacuum survives, with c	o v, \epsilon_0	o\epsilon, \mu_0	o\mu.For a monochromatic plane wave:\omega=kv,the amplitude of \mathbf B is (1/v) times the amplitude of \mathbf E,\mathbf E, \mathbf B, and \hat{\mathbf k} are mutually perpendicular.So for amplitudes,  oxed{B_0= rac{E_0}{v}}. 9.3.1.4 Energy density, Poynting vector, and intensityThe energy density is  oxed{ u= rac12\left(\epsilon E^2+ rac{1}{\mu}B^2ight)} and the Poynting vector is  oxed{ \mathbf S= rac{1}{\mu}(\mathbf E	imes \mathbf B)}. For a monochromatic plane wave, the intensity is  oxed{ I= rac12\,\epsilon v E_0^2 }. So the practical transcription from vacuum is very simple: \epsilon_0 	o \epsilon,\qquad \mu_0 	o \mu,\qquad c 	o v. 9.3.1.5 Key conceptual pointThe mathematics looks simple, but physically this is subtle: the wave polarizes and magnetizes the medium, and the induced microscopic dipoles generate fields that combine with the original wave into one new wave with the same frequency but a different speed.9.3.2 Reflection and Transmission at Normal Incidence9.3.2.1 SetupLet the interface be the xy-plane. A wave in medium 1 travels in the +z direction toward medium 2, polarized in the x direction.The incident wave is  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_I(z,t) &= 	ilde E_{0I} e^{i(k_1 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf x},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_I(z,t) &=  rac{1}{v_1}	ilde E_{0I} e^{i(k_1 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf y}. \end{aligned} The reflected wave is  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_R(z,t) &= 	ilde E_{0R} e^{i(-k_1 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf x},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_R(z,t) &= - rac{1}{v_1}	ilde E_{0R} e^{i(-k_1 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf y}. \end{aligned} The minus sign in 	ilde{\mathbf B}_R is essential: it makes \mathbf E	imes \mathbf B point in the reflected propagation direction.The transmitted wave is  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_T(z,t) &= 	ilde E_{0T} e^{i(k_2 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf x},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_T(z,t) &=  rac{1}{v_2}	ilde E_{0T} e^{i(k_2 z-\omega t)}\hat{\mathbf y}. \end{aligned} 9.3.2.2 Boundary conditionsAt the interface z=0, the fields on the left must match those on the right.For linear media, the boundary conditions are  egin{aligned} \epsilon_1 E_{1}^{\perp} &= \epsilon_2 E_{2}^{\perp}, \ B_{1}^{\perp} &= B_{2}^{\perp}, \ E_{1}^{\parallel} &= E_{2}^{\parallel}, \  rac{1}{\mu_1}B_{1}^{\parallel} &=  rac{1}{\mu_2}B_{2}^{\parallel}. \end{aligned} In the present geometry there are no perpendicular components, so only the tangential conditions matter.From continuity of tangential \mathbf E,  oxed{	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R}=	ilde E_{0T}} and from continuity of tangential \mathbf H=\mathbf B/\mu,  rac{1}{\mu_1}\left( rac{1}{v_1}	ilde E_{0I}- rac{1}{v_1}	ilde E_{0R}ight) =  rac{1}{\mu_2}\left( rac{1}{v_2}	ilde E_{0T}ight). Define  oxed{  eta\equiv  rac{\mu_1 v_1}{\mu_2 v_2} = rac{\mu_1 n_2}{\mu_2 n_1}} so the second condition becomes  oxed{	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R}= eta 	ilde E_{0T}}. 9.3.2.3 Solving for amplitudesNow solve the two equations  egin{aligned} 	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R} &= 	ilde E_{0T},\ 	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R} &=  eta 	ilde E_{0T}. \end{aligned} A clean textbook-style elimination is  egin{aligned} 	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R} &= 	ilde E_{0T},\ 	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R} &=  eta 	ilde E_{0T} \end{aligned} Add and subtract appropriately:  egin{aligned} 2	ilde E_{0I} &=(1+ eta)	ilde E_{0T} \ \Rightarrow\quad 	ilde E_{0T} &= rac{2}{1+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}, \end{aligned} and then  egin{aligned} 	ilde E_{0R} &=	ilde E_{0T}-	ilde E_{0I} \ &=\left( rac{2}{1+ eta}-1ight)	ilde E_{0I} \ &= rac{1- eta}{1+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}. \end{aligned} So  oxed{ 	ilde E_{0R}= rac{1- eta}{1+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}, \qquad 	ilde E_{0T}= rac{2}{1+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}} These are the normal-incidence amplitude relations.9.3.2.4 Common approximation: \mu_1\approx \mu_2For most optical media, \mu_1\approx\mu_2\approx \mu_0, so  eta= rac{v_1}{v_2}. Then  oxed{ 	ilde E_{0R}= rac{v_2-v_1}{v_2+v_1}	ilde E_{0I}, \qquad 	ilde E_{0T}= rac{2v_2}{v_2+v_1}	ilde E_{0I}} or, using v=c/n,  oxed{ E_{0R}=\left| rac{n_1-n_2}{n_1+n_2}ight|E_{0I}, \qquad E_{0T}= rac{2n_1}{n_1+n_2}E_{0I}} with the sign of the reflected amplitude encoding phase reversal:if v_2>v_1 then reflected wave is in phase,if v_2<v_1 then reflected wave is 180^\circ out of phase.9.3.2.5 Reflection and transmission coefficientsSince I= rac12 \epsilon v E_0^2, the reflection coefficient is  oxed{ R\equiv  rac{I_R}{I_I} = \left( rac{E_{0R}}{E_{0I}}ight)^2 = \left( rac{n_1-n_2}{n_1+n_2}ight)^2} and the transmission coefficient is  oxed{ T\equiv  rac{I_T}{I_I} =  rac{\epsilon_2 v_2}{\epsilon_1 v_1} \left( rac{E_{0T}}{E_{0I}}ight)^2 =  rac{4n_1n_2}{(n_1+n_2)^2}} when \mu_1=\mu_2=\mu_0.These satisfy  oxed{R+T=1}, as they must.9.3.2.6 Example / interpretationFor air to glass, n_1=1,\qquad n_2=1.5, so R=0.04,\qquad T=0.96. Only about 4\% is reflected, and most is transmitted.9.3.2.7 Solution technique to rememberFor normal incidence:write incident, reflected, transmitted waves with correct propagation directions,be careful with the sign of \mathbf B_R,apply tangential boundary conditions at the interface,solve the resulting two linear equations for 	ilde E_{0R} and 	ilde E_{0T},convert to intensities using I= rac12 \epsilon v E_0^2.That is the standard template.9.3.3 Reflection and Transmission at Oblique Incidence9.3.3.1 General setupNow let the incident wave strike the interface at angle 	heta_I.Write  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_I(\mathbf r,t)&=	ilde{\mathbf E}_{0I}e^{i(\mathbf k_I\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_I(\mathbf r,t)&= rac{1}{v_1}(\hat{\mathbf k}_I	imes 	ilde{\mathbf E}_I), \end{aligned}  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_R(\mathbf r,t)&=	ilde{\mathbf E}_{0R}e^{i(\mathbf k_R\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_R(\mathbf r,t)&= rac{1}{v_1}(\hat{\mathbf k}_R	imes 	ilde{\mathbf E}_R), \end{aligned}  egin{aligned} 	ilde{\mathbf E}_T(\mathbf r,t)&=	ilde{\mathbf E}_{0T}e^{i(\mathbf k_T\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)},\ 	ilde{\mathbf B}_T(\mathbf r,t)&= rac{1}{v_2}(\hat{\mathbf k}_T	imes 	ilde{\mathbf E}_T). \end{aligned} All three waves have the same frequency \omega, while the wave numbers satisfy  oxed{ k_I v_1=k_R v_1=k_T v_2=\omega} so  oxed{ k_I=k_R= rac{v_2}{v_1}k_T= rac{n_1}{n_2}k_T}. 9.3.3.2 Matching the phase along the boundaryAt z=0, the boundary conditions have the generic form (\cdots)e^{i(\mathbf k_I\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)} + (\cdots)e^{i(\mathbf k_R\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)} = (\cdots)e^{i(\mathbf k_T\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)}. Because this must hold for all points on the interface and for all time, the exponential factors must match when z=0:  oxed{ \mathbf k_I\cdot\mathbf r = \mathbf k_R\cdot\mathbf r = \mathbf k_T\cdot\mathbf r \qquad (z=0)} which implies equality of the components parallel to the interface.9.3.3.3 The three laws of geometrical opticsChoose axes so that the incident wave lies in the xz plane. Then the reflected and transmitted wave vectors must also lie in that plane.So:9.3.3.3.1 First lawThe incident, reflected, and transmitted wave vectors all lie in a single plane, the plane of incidence, which also contains the normal to the surface.From matching tangential components, k_I\sin	heta_I=k_R\sin	heta_R=k_T\sin	heta_T. Using k_I=k_R, we get9.3.3.3.2 Second law: reflection  oxed{	heta_I=	heta_R} and using k_I/k_T=n_1/n_2, we get9.3.3.3.3 Third law: refraction (Snell’s law)  oxed{  rac{\sin	heta_T}{\sin	heta_I}= rac{n_1}{n_2}} or equivalently  oxed{n_1\sin	heta_I=n_2\sin	heta_T}. A key point is that these laws follow mostly from phase matching, before using the detailed electromagnetic boundary conditions.9.3.3.4 Boundary conditions for amplitudesAfter the exponentials cancel, the boundary conditions become relations among amplitudes:  egin{aligned} \epsilon_1(	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R})_z &= \epsilon_2(	ilde E_{0T})_z,\ (	ilde B_{0I}+	ilde B_{0R})_z &= (	ilde B_{0T})_z,\ (	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R})_{x,y} &= (	ilde E_{0T})_{x,y},\  rac{1}{\mu_1}(	ilde B_{0I}+	ilde B_{0R})_{x,y} &=  rac{1}{\mu_2}(	ilde B_{0T})_{x,y}. \end{aligned} At this point polarization matters.9.3.3.5 Case 1: polarization parallel to the plane of incidenceTake the incident electric field in the plane of incidence (xz-plane). Then the reflected and transmitted fields are also in that plane.The boundary conditions reduce to \epsilon_1(-	ilde E_{0I}\sin	heta_I+	ilde E_{0R}\sin	heta_R) = \epsilon_2(-	ilde E_{0T}\sin	heta_T), 	ilde E_{0I}\cos	heta_I+	ilde E_{0R}\cos	heta_R = 	ilde E_{0T}\cos	heta_T,  rac{1}{\mu_1 v_1}(	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R}) =  rac{1}{\mu_2 v_2}	ilde E_{0T}. Using the laws of reflection and refraction, the first and third both reduce to  oxed{	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R}= eta 	ilde E_{0T}} with  oxed{ eta= rac{\mu_1 v_1}{\mu_2 v_2} = rac{\mu_1 n_2}{\mu_2 n_1}} while the second becomes  oxed{	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R}=\alpha 	ilde E_{0T}} where  oxed{\alpha\equiv  rac{\cos	heta_T}{\cos	heta_I}}. Now solve exactly as in the normal-incidence case:  egin{aligned} 	ilde E_{0I}-	ilde E_{0R}&= eta 	ilde E_{0T},\ 	ilde E_{0I}+	ilde E_{0R}&=\alpha 	ilde E_{0T}. \end{aligned} Hence  oxed{ 	ilde E_{0R}= rac{\alpha- eta}{\alpha+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}, \qquad 	ilde E_{0T}= rac{2}{\alpha+ eta}	ilde E_{0I}} These are the Fresnel equations for polarization parallel to the plane of incidence.9.3.3.6 Angular dependence and Brewster angleHere \alpha= rac{\cos	heta_T}{\cos	heta_I} =  rac{\sqrt{1-\sin^2	heta_T}}{\cos	heta_I} =  rac{\sqrt{1-\left[(n_1/n_2)\sin	heta_Iight]^2}}{\cos	heta_I}. So the reflected and transmitted amplitudes depend on the incident angle.A remarkable feature is the existence of Brewster’s angle 	heta_B, where the reflected wave disappears: 	ilde E_{0R}=0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \alpha= eta. This yields  oxed{ \sin^2	heta_B= rac{1- eta^2}{(n_1/n_2)^2- eta^2}} and for the common case \mu_1\approx \mu_2,  eta\approx  rac{n_2}{n_1} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  oxed{	an	heta_B\approx  rac{n_2}{n_1}}. At this angle, the reflected beam is extinguished for this polarization.9.3.3.7 Intensities for the parallel-polarization caseThe power per unit area of interface is the normal component of the Poynting vector, so  oxed{ I_I= rac12 \epsilon_1 v_1 E_{0I}^2 \cos	heta_I}  oxed{ I_R= rac12 \epsilon_1 v_1 E_{0R}^2 \cos	heta_R, \qquad I_T= rac12 \epsilon_2 v_2 E_{0T}^2 \cos	heta_T} The extra cosine factors appear because the wave fronts are tilted relative to the interface.So the reflection and transmission coefficients are  oxed{ R= rac{I_R}{I_I} = \left( rac{E_{0R}}{E_{0I}}ight)^2 = \left( rac{\alpha- eta}{\alpha+ eta}ight)^2} and  oxed{ T= rac{I_T}{I_I} =  rac{\epsilon_2 v_2}{\epsilon_1 v_1} \left( rac{E_{0T}}{E_{0I}}ight)^2  rac{\cos	heta_T}{\cos	heta_I} = \alpha eta\left( rac{2}{\alpha+ eta}ight)^2} with  oxed{R+T=1}. At Brewster’s angle, R=0,\qquad T=1. 9.3.3.8 Case 2: polarization perpendicular to the plane of incidenceGriffiths leaves the full derivation to the problem set. The important conceptual takeaway is:there is another pair of Fresnel equations for this polarization,unlike the parallel-polarization case, there is no Brewster angle in the generic case,the reflected component is not extinguished.So polarization is not a minor detail; it changes the boundary-value problem.9.3.3.9 Solution technique to remember for oblique incidenceThe standard Griffiths strategy is:write incident, reflected, and transmitted plane waves in complex notation,impose equality of phase along the interface,deduce:coplanarity of \mathbf k_I,\mathbf k_R,\mathbf k_T,	heta_I=	heta_R,Snell’s law,then impose amplitude boundary conditions,solve separately for each polarization.This is the cleanest way to organize the algebra.9.3.4 Summary sheet9.3.4.1 Wave propagation in matter  oxed{ 
